.ft B
The bunkbed conjecture for transitive tournaments
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
.sp 1v
.sp 0.65v
Draft prepared with AI assistance; authorship to be determined.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Draft manuscript; not peer reviewed.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
Abstract
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
We prove the bunkbed conjecture in Hollom's E7 model for the transitive tournament T(n): for every n and all vertices u, v, the probability that (u,0) reaches (v,0) is at least the probability that (u,0) reaches (v,1). The proof reveals the random tokens column by column, compresses the revealed prefix to the pair of reachable-copy counts, and preserves a reflected mass dominance by a paired-sum induction. Transitivity is used through the total order, which prevents a path from returning to a smaller index, and through completeness, which makes every earlier vertex a predecessor of the new one. The endpoint is kernel checked in Lean 4.34.1 with mathlib revision d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612; the reported axioms are exactly propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound. No peer review, priority or worldwide novelty is claimed.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
1. Introduction
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
The bunkbed conjecture concerns two copies, called layers, of a graph, joined by vertical edges. In the undirected form, with independent retention of edges, it asserts that for all vertices u and v the probability that the layer-0 copies of u and v are connected is at least the probability that the layer-0 copy of u is connected to the layer-1 copy of v. The conjecture was introduced by Kasteleyn in 1985 and is now known to be false in general (Gladkov, Pak and Zimin).
.br
.sp 0.65v
Hollom studied directed versions. In the model E7 of the arXiv version, renamed D7 in the published journal version at the same numbered locations, a directed graph is doubled; each horizontal arc is retained independently in each layer, while each vertex carries one vertical token that enables both directions at once. Hollom's Theorem 1.6 shows that BBC(D7(D)) fails for suitable directed multigraphs. He then asks, in Question 6.3, whether BBC(D7(D)) holds for all acyclic digraphs, and writes that it "seems unclear ... whether the answer ... should be positive or negative", adding that the problem is "far more amenable to inductive techniques than the case of graphs with cycles, but actually performing this induction seems far from trivial".
.br
.sp 0.65v
Conjecture 6.4 then singles out the transitive tournament: it asserts that BBC(D7(T(n))) holds for T(n), the transitive tournament on n vertices. Hollom calls this "one case that does seem approachable, though was found by the author to be more difficult than expected", and remarks that the techniques used for the undirected complete graph "do not directly apply, in short because Tn does not have the same symmetries as Kn". The undirected complete graph case is due to van Hintum and Lammers.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The general acyclic question was later answered negatively by Przybylowski: he constructs a simple acyclic directed graph with distinguished vertices u and v such that P(u- \(-> v-) < P(u- \(-> v+) (Theorem 1.2), resolving the conjectures of Leander and Hollom. His graph has missing adjacencies and is not a tournament, so it does not contradict Conjecture 6.4. Tournaments are singled out because the order is total: every earlier vertex is a predecessor of every later one. In the proof below, this completeness is exactly what makes the pair of reachable-copy counts a sufficient statistic. The MathDB record 382297 states the same conjecture for T(n) in the E7 model and records it as open.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
2. Model and theorem
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
Let T(n) be the transitive tournament with vertex set {0,...,n-1}, in which i \(-> j is an arc exactly when i < j. The bunkbed has vertices (i,l) with i in {0,...,n-1} and layer l in {0,1}. There are two kinds of random token:
.br
.sp 0.4v
.in +0.3i
\(bu For every arc i \(-> j and every layer l there is one fair horizontal token h(i,j,l). If h(i,j,l) is retained, the arc (i,l) \(-> (j,l) is present.
.br
.sp 0.3v
\(bu For every index i there is one fair vertical token z(i). If z(i) is retained, both arcs (i,0) \(-> (i,1) and (i,1) \(-> (i,0) are present. The two directions are enabled together by the single token.
.br
.in -0.3i
.sp 0.4v
An outcome is an assignment of 0 or 1 to every token, and all 2^(n(n-1)+n) = 2^(n*n) outcomes are equally likely. Reachability is the existence of a directed path; formally it is the reflexive-transitive closure of the enabled-arc relation, so the zero-length path counts and every vertex reaches itself. We write (u,0) \(-> (v,s) when (v,s) is reachable from (u,0).
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
Theorem 2.1.
.ft R
For every n \(>= 0 and all u, v in {0,...,n-1},
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
P((u,0) \(-> (v,0)) \(>= P((u,0) \(-> (v,1)).
.fi
.sp 0.3v
Equivalently, in the finite uniform model,
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
|{\(*w : Reach n \(*w (u,false) (v,true)}| \(<= |{\(*w : Reach n \(*w (u,false) (v,false)}|,
.fi
.sp 0.3v
where both sets are counted among the 2^(n*n) outcomes; the common denominator cancels. For n = 0 there are no vertices u and v, so the statement is vacuous.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
3. Proof
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
Fix u. Since every arc weakly increases the index, a directed path from (u,0) never visits an index below u; in particular, if v < u then both events are impossible. We therefore reveal the tokens in increasing index order and track reachability of the revealed prefix.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
3.1 Prefixes and counts.
.ft R
For k \(>= u, let P(k) be the set of tokens whose largest touched index is at most k, that is, the horizontal tokens of arcs into indices at most k together with the vertical tokens of indices at most k. Reveal P(u), P(u+1), ... in order. Let R0(k) and R1(k) be the sets of indices in {u,...,k} whose layer-0 and layer-1 copies are reachable from (u,0) using only the tokens of P(k), and put
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
A(k) = |R0(k)|,    B(k) = |R1(k)|.
.fi
.sp 0.3v
Reachability of a vertex of index at most k is the same whether we use P(k) or all tokens: any directed path to such a vertex has nondecreasing indices, so it stays in {u,...,k} and uses only tokens whose largest touched index is at most k. Thus the sets R0(k) and R1(k) are final for their indices; later tokens cannot change them. This is the no-return property, and it uses only acyclicity.
.br
.sp 0.65v
At k = u the prefix P(u) contains the vertical token at u and the horizontal tokens into u from lower indices. The latter cannot be used, because no index below u is reachable from (u,0). The zero-length path makes u belong to R0(u) always, and the copy (u,1) is reachable exactly when z(u) is retained. Hence
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
(A(u),B(u)) = (1,1) with probability 1/2,   and   (1,0) with probability 1/2.
.fi
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
3.2 The increment law.
.ft R
Condition on the whole revealed prefix P(k), and suppose its counts are (a,b). The new index is j = k+1. A path first enters index j horizontally from a previously reachable copy at some i < j, because no vertex of index j is reachable before index j is entered; after the first entry, the only further vertex at index j is the vertical partner. Let X be the indicator that at least one bottom arc from R0(k) to (j,0) is retained, and let Y be the indicator that at least one top arc from R1(k) to (j,1) is retained. These two families contain exactly a and b arcs respectively, they are disjoint because they lie in different layers, and all their tokens are independent and fair. Hence X and Y are independent with
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
P(X = 0) = 2^(-a),    P(Y = 0) = 2^(-b).
.fi
.sp 0.3v
Let Z be the vertical token at j, independent and fair. The new reachable indicators are
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
U = X or (Z and Y)   for layer 0,
V = Y or (Z and X)   for layer 1,
.fi
.sp 0.3v
and the counts become (a+U, b+V). This is exact: if X = 0 and ZY = 0 there is no entry into (j,0), and if X = 1 there is one; if X = 0 but Y = 1 and Z = 1, the vertical token carries reachability from (j,1) to (j,0). The same reasoning gives V. There is no other route into index j, because arcs enter j only from lower indices or vertically from the partner at j, and no path returns to the old prefix.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The law of (X,Y,Z) depends on the prefix only through a and b. This is the point at which completeness in the order is used: every index i < j is a predecessor of j, so R0(k) contributes exactly a candidate bottom arcs and R1(k) exactly b candidate top arcs, no matter which indices belong to these sets. All these arcs are present independently with probability 1/2, and the tokens in the two layers are distinct. If some pairs i < j were missing, the number of candidate arcs into j would depend on the identity of R0(k), not merely on its size.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Write q = 2^(-a) and r = 2^(-b). The four possible increments have probabilities
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
P(U=0,V=0) = q r,
P(U=1,V=0) = (1-q) r / 2,
P(U=0,V=1) = q (1-r) / 2,
P(U=1,V=1) = 1 - (q+r)/2.
.fi
.sp 0.3v
They sum to one. For example, (U,V) = (1,0) means that a bottom entry exists, no top entry exists, and Z = 0, which has probability (1-q)r/2; the case (1,1) is the complement of the other three.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
3.3 Reflection and reflected mass dominance.
.ft R
Let \(*r(a,b) = (b,a) be reflection in the diagonal. The increment kernel K satisfies K(\(*r s, \(*r t) = K(s,t): swapping X and Y and keeping Z exchanges the two middle probabilities and fixes the other two. Also U and V are bits, so the difference a-b changes by at most one in a single step. Therefore a one-step transition from a > b cannot end at a < b, and a one-step transition from a < b cannot end at a > b. This is the no-crossing property.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Let \(*m(k) be the law of (A(k),B(k)). For a finitely supported probability \(*m on pairs (a,b), define
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
\(*D\(*m(a,b) = \(*m(a,b) - \(*m(b,a).
.fi
.sp 0.3v
We claim that \(*D\(*m(k)(a,b) \(>= 0 whenever a > b. Initially \(*m(u) is 1/2 at (1,0) and 1/2 at (1,1); for (1,0) we have \(*D = 1/2 - 0 = 1/2 \(>= 0, and (1,1) is diagonal. For the inductive step, let \(*m be a law with \(*D\(*m(a,b) \(>= 0 for a > b, and let t = (a,b) with a > b. Then
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
\(*D(\(*mK)(t) = \(*S_s \(*m(s) [K(s,t) - K(s,\(*r t)]
             = \(*S_{s above} \(*D\(*m(s) [K(s,t) - K(s,\(*r t)]
             = \(*S_{s above} \(*D\(*m(s) K(s,t) \(>= 0.
.fi
.sp 0.3v
The second equality pairs each state s above the diagonal with its reflection \(*r s; diagonal states cancel because reflection fixes them and K(s,\(*r t) = K(s,t) when \(*r s = s. The third equality uses no crossing: s is above the diagonal and \(*r t is below it, so K(s,\(*r t) = 0. All sums are finite. Thus reflected mass dominance is preserved at every prefix. We do not claim that A(k) \(>= B(k) holds pathwise; only the signed mass inequality is asserted.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
3.4 The payoff.
.ft R
Conditionally on (a,b), we have U - V = (1-Z)(X-Y), hence
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
g(a,b) := E[U-V | a,b] = (1/2)(2^(-b) - 2^(-a)).
.fi
.sp 0.3v
The function g is antisymmetric, g(b,a) = -g(a,b); it vanishes on the diagonal and is nonnegative above it, because a > b gives 2^(-a) < 2^(-b). For any law \(*m with \(*D\(*m(a,b) \(>= 0 for a > b,
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
E_{\(*m}[U-V] = \(*S_s \(*m(s) g(s) = \(*S_{a>b} \(*D\(*m(a,b) g(a,b) \(>= 0,
.fi
.sp 0.3v
by pairing s with \(*r s and using g(\(*r s) = -g(s) and g = 0 on the diagonal.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
3.5 The endpoint.
.ft R
Fix v > u. The law \(*m(v-1) of (A(v-1),B(v-1)) satisfies reflected mass dominance by Section 3.3. At the step into index v, the indicators U and V are exactly the indicators that v belongs to R0(v) and R1(v). Therefore
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
P((u,0) \(-> (v,0)) - P((u,0) \(-> (v,1))
  = E[U-V] = E_{\(*m(v-1)}[g(A,B)] \(>= 0.
.fi
.sp 0.3v
The equality with the full connection probabilities holds because reachability of index v is decided by the prefix through v: every path to v has nondecreasing indices and ends at v, so it uses only tokens whose largest touched index is at most v.
.br
.sp 0.65v
If v < u, both events are impossible by index monotonicity, so both probabilities are zero. If v = u, the zero-length path gives probability 1 for (u,0) \(-> (u,0), while (u,0) \(-> (u,1) requires the vertical token at u and has probability 1/2; thus 1 \(>= 1/2. Together with the vacuous case n = 0, this covers all n and all u, v, proving Theorem 2.1.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
3.6 Where transitivity is used.
.ft R
Transitivity enters at two points. First, horizontal arcs strictly increase the index and vertical arcs keep it, so no path returns to a smaller index; this makes prefix reachability final and permits the column-by-column revelation. Second, and indispensably, every pair i < j is an arc, so every reachable lower copy contributes exactly one candidate arc into the new copy. This completeness is what makes the candidate counts equal to a and b, and hence what makes the counts a sufficient statistic.
.br
.sp 0.65v
A general acyclic digraph has the first property but not the second. There, the number of candidate arcs into a new vertex is the number of its reachable predecessors, which depends on which vertices are reachable, not merely on how many. For instance, if a later vertex j has only one predecessor x, then a prefix that reaches x0 but not y0 and a prefix that reaches y0 but not x0 have the same counts, yet different entry probabilities. The count process is then not Markov in (a,b), and the paired-sum induction has no analogue. Przybylowski's acyclic counterexample shows that the statement itself fails in that generality. Adding the missing arcs to obtain a tournament changes the connection probabilities, and no monotonicity of their difference under edge addition is available.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
4. Remarks
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
4.1 Column-homogeneous probabilities.
.ft R
The same proof works under a broader law, which we record as an immediate extension and not as a novelty claim. Fix any set T of posts, and for each column j let the horizontal tokens of all arcs i \(-> j in both layers be retained independently with a common probability p(j) in [0,1]. Let Z(j) = 1 if j is in T; the vertical token at j is retained exactly when Z(j) = 1. Then the failure probabilities at a prefix with counts (a,b) are q(j)^a and q(j)^b, where q(j) = 1 - p(j), and the conditional payoff is
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
g(j)(a,b) = (1 - Z(j)) (q(j)^b - q(j)^a).
.fi
.sp 0.3v
The kernel is time-inhomogeneous, but reflection equivariance and no crossing still hold at every step, and the initial state is (1,1) if u is in T and (1,0) otherwise. The paired-sum induction therefore gives the same inequality. Conditioning on an arbitrary random post set is also allowed; taking each vertex independently in T with probability 1/2 recovers E7, with p(j) = 1/2 for all j. Columns with p(j) = 0, which have no incoming horizontal arc, are included. This extension does not cover arbitrary posets, interval orders or general acyclic digraphs, for the reason given in Section 3.6.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
4.2 Reflection until meeting.
.ft R
The paired-sum induction is a reflection-until-meeting argument, and reflection until meeting is an old technique (Aldous and Fill, Chapter 12, Section 12.1.8). We make no novelty claim for it. The process here is two-dimensional, with state-dependent transition probabilities and a terminal antisymmetric payoff; it is not the cycle walk treated in that reference.
.br
.bp
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
5. Lean certificate
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.5v
.sp 0.65v
The development uses Lean 4.34.1 with mathlib revision d13f23b723b8a846827a245b89c10fc7d3f11612. It defines the literal model: HToken carries i < j and a layer; Token is the disjoint sum of HToken and Fin n; Out is Token \(-> Bool; Arc is the enabled-arc relation; and Reach is Relation.ReflTransGen. The endpoint is stated in cardinality form, in namespace Bun, so its full name is Bun.bunkbed_E7_transitive:
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
.ft CR
.ps 9
.vs 11
theorem bunkbed_E7_transitive (n : Nat) (u v : Fin n) :
    Fintype.card {\(*w : Out n // Reach n \(*w (u, false) (v, true)} \(<=
    Fintype.card {\(*w : Out n // Reach n \(*w (u, false) (v, false)} := by
.fi
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
.sp 0.3v
The audit prints the same type, with standard natural-number notation in place of Nat. The model checks are stated exactly as in the source:
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
.ft CR
.ps 9
.vs 11
theorem card_token (n : Nat) : Fintype.card (Token n) = n * n := by
theorem card_out (n : Nat) : Fintype.card (Out n) = 2 ^ (n * n) := by
.fi
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
.sp 0.3v
Thus there are n(n-1) horizontal tokens, two per arc, plus n vertical tokens: n*n tokens and 2^(n*n) outcomes. The rational-valued probability form is recorded as Bun.prob_layer_true_le_false.
.br
.sp 0.65v
The axiom audit reports that Bun.bunkbed_E7_transitive depends on exactly [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound]. The same three appear for the main auxiliary results, and Bun.reach_of_lt depends only on [propext, Quot.sound]. Nothing else appears: there is no sorry, admit, added axiom, unsafe or native_decide. The assembly record states that 33 modules were rebuilt from an empty build, with source hashes unchanged.
.br
.sp 0.65v
Two bridges carry the ordinary argument into the literal model. The Reach bridge is Bun.stf_reach_endpoint, with strong form Bun.stf_reach_strong: membership of v in the layer-\(*s component of the prefix state stf n u v (wres n v \(*w) is equivalent to literal Reach n \(*w (u,false) (v,\(*s), and the strong form adds the guard u \(<= i \(<= j. The state-level inequality is Bun.state_endpoint:
.br
.sp 0.3v
.nf
.ft CR
.ps 9
.vs 11
theorem state_endpoint : \(fa (n u j : Nat), u \(<= j \(-> j < n \(-> 
    Fintype.card {w : Bun.Asg n j // j \(mo Bun.sval true (Bun.stf n u j w)} \(<=
    Fintype.card {w : Bun.Asg n j // j \(mo Bun.sval false (Bun.stf n u j w)} := by
.fi
.ft R
.ps 11
.vs 14
.sp 0.3v
The counting bridge Bun.window_factor rewrites each full-outcome count as 2^(tokens outside the window) times the corresponding window-assignment count, so both sides share a common factor. The endpoint proof splits into v < u, where both events are empty by Bun.reach_of_lt, and u \(<= v, where the two bridges and Nat.mul_le_mul_left finish. No hypothesis is added and no statement is weakened.
.br
.sp 0.65v
.ne 4v
.ft B
Independent audit.
.ft R
A separate reviewer extracted the 33 modules from a sealed archive, matched their hashes, and compiled and kernel-checked all of them from an empty build. The reviewer read every module and found no sorry, added axiom, unsafe code or metaprogramming, and no hypothesis carrying the conclusion. A walk of the 6913 constants in the transitive closure of the endpoint found only the three axioms above and no unsafe or partial constant. The statement was compared with Hollom's definitions and the MathDB record. Lean control modules confirm the exact counts 1 and 2 at n = 1, the counts 7 and 9 out of 16 for a case at n = 2, that the reversed inequality is false for some instance, and that both vertical directions share one token. Four cosmetic or low-severity defects were reported, none affecting validity or fidelity. The relabeling of an abstract transitive tournament to the vertex set {0,...,n-1} is not formalized and is trivial. Upstream Lean and mathlib were trusted rather than rebuilt, and the mathlib checkout was confirmed to be at the stated revision. The reviewer was an AI system distinct from those that produced the proof, and this is not a human referee report.
.br
.sp 1v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
6. Limits
.br
.ft R
.ps 11
.sp 0.3v
.sp 0.3v
The prior-art search was bounded: it covered the supplied primary sources and a bounded later-literature search, not a full citation graph, and does not establish worldwide absence. The final journal version of Przybylowski's paper (DOI 10.1016/j.ejc.2025.104289) was not read; the supplied version is not final-equivalence evidence. The proof is short and elementary, and the reflection device is old. The column-homogeneous extension is immediate. No peer review, priority or worldwide novelty is claimed. This draft was prepared with AI assistance.
.br
.sp 0.3v
.ne 5v
.ft B
.ps 13
